;; Docs for std/stream.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "stream.bjodoc"). The
;; texts are samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Loops over values that suspending work produces: streams, and channels read until an end.")
  (see Stream make-stream emit! streaming in-chan)
  (example """
    (import (std stream))

    ;; Pages, fetched one at a time as the loop wants them.
    (def pages {streaming (fetch url) (:for url urls)})
    {taking 10 page (:for page pages)}     ; fetches ten pages, then stops
    """)
  (example """
    ;; A producer written by hand.
    (def ticks
      (make-stream (bjoroutine (out)
                     (loop (:for i (range 0 3))
                           (:do (sync (timeout (seconds 1))) (emit! out i)))))))
    (loop (:for t ticks) (:do (println (int->string t))))
    """)
  (reference """
    A @code{Seq} cannot suspend: it is a .NET iterator, and an iterator
    cannot also await. A @code{Stream} is the sequence for that case. Its
    producer runs in a fiber of its own and hands each value over a channel,
    so the producer can wait for anything, and the loop over it waits for the
    producer.

    @strong{The loop owns the producer.} A stream is a recipe. Holding one
    runs nothing. Each loop over it starts the producer, and stops it when the
    loop ends: when the producer is done, at a @code{:finish} or an
    @code{:abandon}, when a driver beside it runs out, at @code{finding} or
    @code{taking}, at an exception or a @code{ret}. A producer stopped so raises
    @code{Cancelled} where it waits, and its own releases run. It unwinds in
    its own fiber: the loop does not wait for that, the enclosing scope does.
    What the
    producer raises for its own reasons is raised in the loop. A loop at an
    inner level gets a producer each time the level starts.

    @strong{Nothing is computed ahead.} The producer computes a value only
    when the loop asks for it: after each @code{emit!} it waits for the next
    request. So a loop left early has cost exactly the values it took, and
    @code{port->stream} in @code{(std ports)} leaves the port just after the
    last item taken. One exception: a driver beside the stream that runs out
    is seen after the stream has stepped, so that costs one value more.

    @strong{A channel somebody else fills} is read with @code{in-chan}, which
    ends when an event commits, such as the producer's
    @code{promise-join}. That loop does not own the producer: leaving it
    early leaves the producer and the channel as they are, for another
    reader.

    @strong{Where the loop runs.} In a bjoroutine, each receive suspends. In
    ordinary code it parks the thread, as @code{sync/blocking} does.
    """))

(:doc Stream
  (summary "A sequence whose values a producer that may suspend emits. Each loop over it runs the producer afresh.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the values.")
  (see make-stream streaming emit! Iterable)
  (reference """
    An @code{Iterable}: @code{(:for x s)} in a loop or a comprehension. Its
    cursor is a @code{StreamRun}, made when the loop starts.
    """))

(:doc Emitter
  (summary "Where a stream's producer sends its values, with emit!.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the values.")
  (see emit! make-stream))

(:doc StreamRun
  (summary "A loop's cursor over a stream: the running producer, and the value the loop is at.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the values.")
  (see Stream)
  (reference """
    Made by @code{iterable-start} and ended by @code{iterable-end} or
    @code{iterable-cleanup}, which stop the producer.
    """))

(:doc Item
  (summary "What a stream's producer hands the loop: a value, the end, or what the producer raised.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the values.")
  (case Next "A value.")
  (case Done "The producer returned.")
  (case Failed "The producer raised this. The loop raises it again.")
  (see Stream))

(:doc ChanReads
  (summary "The values received on a channel until an event commits: what in-chan makes.")
  (tparam %a "The type of the values.")
  (tparam %e "The type of the end event's value, which is dropped.")
  (see in-chan))

(:doc make-stream
  (summary "A stream whose producer is body.")
  (arg body "A bjoroutine given an Emitter. It emits each value with emit!, and the stream ends when it returns.")
  (returns "The stream. Nothing runs until a loop over it starts.")
  (see emit! streaming Stream)
  (example """
    (make-stream (bjoroutine (out)
                   (emit! out 1)
                   (emit! out 2)))
    """))

(:doc emit!
  (summary "Hands a value to the loop over the stream, and waits until the loop asks for the next one.")
  (arg out "The producer's Emitter.")
  (arg v "The value.")
  (raises Bjolang.Runtime.Cancelled "When the loop has ended. The producer then unwinds.")
  (see make-stream)
  (reference """
    The producer goes on only when the loop asks for another value, so it
    computes nothing the loop does not take.
    """))

(:doc in-chan
  (summary "The values received on a channel, until an event commits.")
  (arg c "The channel.")
  (arg end "The event that ends the reading, such as (promise-join producer) or (timeout d).")
  (returns "A ChanReads, for (:for x (in-chan c end)).")
  (see Stream promise-join chan-recv)
  (example """
    (def feeder (bjo (feed jobs urls)))
    (loop (:for url (in-chan jobs (promise-join feeder)))
          (:do (fetch url)))
    """)
  (reference """
    Each step receives from @code{c} or waits for @code{end}, whichever
    commits first. A channel is a rendezvous, so a producer that has
    returned has handed over every value it sent: its promise is a safe end.

    The loop does not own the producer. Leaving it early stops the reading
    only; what is not read is still there for another reader.
    """))

(:doc streaming
  (summary "A stream of an expression for each round of a loop, which runs in the producer and may suspend.")
  (form (streaming expr clause ...))
  (arg expr "The value emitted each round.")
  (rest clause "Loop clauses, as in a comprehension.")
  (see Stream make-stream)
  (example """
    {streaming (fetch url) (:for url urls)}
    {streaming i :when (odd? i) (:for i (range 0 10))}
    """)
  (reference """
    Written as a comprehension, @code{{streaming expr clause ...}}. It is
    @code{seqing} for work that suspends: the loop of the clauses runs in the
    stream's producer and emits @code{expr} each round, and a loose
    @code{:when} gates it.
    """))
